  "final_text": "All four steps are done. The rule was created enabled, so no enable call was needed, and reading it back confirms `enabled: true`.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (ID `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`, index `kibana_sample_data_flights`). Its time field is `timestamp`. The main fields are `Carrier`, `Cancelled` (boolean), `FlightDelay`, `FlightDelayMin`, `DestCountry`, `Origin`/`Dest` variants, `AvgTicketPrice`, `DistanceKilometers`/`DistanceMiles`, `FlightTimeMin`/`FlightTimeHour`, `OriginLocation`/`DestLocation` (geo_point), `dayOfWeek`, and `hour_of_day`.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-d3fdc776 Flights' (ID `58935d0d-61dd-4a62-bfd1-5d04afc588f7`), with exactly two panels built on the flights data view:\n   - \"Flights over time\": line chart, count over a `timestamp` date histogram.\n   - \"Number of flights by carrier\" (renamed in step 4): bar chart, count split by `Carrier` terms, top 10.\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-d3fdc776 Cancelled flights' (ID `94ed9f4f-95bd-4819-a8fc-fada433a1824`). It is an Elasticsearch query rule (`.es-query`) on the flights data view with KQL `Cancelled : true`, a count aggregation, and a 5-minute window. It fires when the count is greater than 10 and runs every 1m. I chose KQL and no action connectors, since the task didn't name any. The rule has no actions, so it records alerts but sends no notifications.\n\n4. **Panel rename:** the carrier panel is now titled 'Flights by carrier'. I saved it with a GET followed by a PUT, changing only that title. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`, with execution status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
